 message which is  exactly why i think people were so  incredibly unhappy with its ending  and while naruto was clearly the  strongest ninja in the end  was he really the one that best  represented the theme of the story  with luffy oda chose the most  interesting and relevant character when  it comes to exploring  freedom luffy is the story  literally also i think now would be a  really good time to subscribe to the  channel one moment there  what about all those weird looking  villains that one piece has that guy  they're  so not badass  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  but not only luffy embodies the central  moral arguments  all of the villains and antagonists that  he faces along the way  as diverse as they might look and as  different their ambitions might be  all of them without any exception i can  think of stand  opposed to the ideal of freedom in some  way or form  villains like crocodile and doflamingo  who control and manipulate entire  countries  villains like caesar who capture  children to experiment on  or magellan who literally runs a prison  villains like the tenryuubito will  literally enslave people and treat them  as objects  and even villains like katakuri who is  unfree to be himself and suppressed by  the high expectations of his family  let me tell you this consistency in the  main values and ideals that are clashing  in the story  is such a fundamental aspect of  storytelling  but really hard to do right and yet  neither the antagonists nor luffy ever  do  anything that really feels out of  character and strives away from these  ideals  this consistency represents luffy in  such a clear and  tangible way that it makes him a very  three-dimensional and very authentic  character  but even in his function as a main  protagonist luffy differs from the trope  of the typical shonen hero  typically we have the main character  that starts somewhere at the bottom  gives us the typical coming of age type  of story arc where the protagonist is  the ou